In visual navigation tasks, memories can be beneficial but also lead to information overload. To address this issue, we've developed a method called MemoNav that learns to select relevant memories from a set of stored experiences. Our approach is based on the assumption that memories with similar features to the current state are more likely to be useful. Experimental results demonstrate that MemoNav effectively retrieves memories and outperforms existing methods in navigation tasks. Additionally, we've introduced a new dataset called MemNav-80, which features navigation challenges with memory retrieval options. Our code and dataset are available for public use. 

